    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The clock that wraps"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "It is 9 o'clock now. What time will it be 5 hours later? Not 14 o'clock on a 12-hour clock - it is 2. The clock wraps around, and that wrapping is modular arithmetic."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "congruence",
          "17 = 5 (mod 12)",
          "same remainder",
          "remainder",
          "add and multiply",
          "negative numbers",
          "uses"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Let a, b be integers and m a positive integer. We say a is congruent to b modulo m, written a = b (mod m), if m divides a - b.\nSo 17 = 5 (mod 12), because 12 divides 17 - 5 = 12.\nAnother way: a and b leave the same remainder when divided by m.\nTo find a (mod m), divide a by m and take the remainder: 100 (mod 7) = 2 because 100 = 7 x 14 + 2.\nCongruences can be added and multiplied: if a = b and c = d (mod m) then a + c = b + d and ac = bd (mod m).\nNegative numbers also work: -3 = 4 (mod 7), because 7 divides -3 - 4 = -7.\nReal uses: days of the week (mod 7), clocks (mod 12 or 24) and check digits in bar codes."
        }
      },
